  "P0044": {"title":"HO2S heater control circuit high Bank 1 Sensor 3","causes":"Heater control circuit voltage higher than expected: heater control wire shorted to voltage, open ground or element, or a module driver fault.","symptoms":"Usually a warning light only; slower closed-loop fuel control is possible.","checks":"Measure heater element resistance (infinite = open element), check the heater ground and the control wire for a short to power.","repair":"Repair damaged wiring or connector. If the heater resistance is out of specification and the circuit tests good, replace the sensor/heater using the correct part for that position. Clear the code and verify heater operation.","prevention":"Keep connectors dry and clean, repair chafed wiring early, and follow the maintenance schedule.","note":"Exhaust parts get extremely hot; let them cool before handling. A code points to a circuit or system; it does not prove the named part has failed. Test before replacing parts. Confirm the exact definition for the vehicle with OEM service information.","type":"generic"},
  "P0045": {"title":"Turbocharger/supercharger boost control solenoid A circuit/open","causes":"Open circuit: blown fuse, open supply or control wire, poor connector contact, open coil/winding, or a failed module driver. Boost leaks, a sticking wastegate or actuator vacuum supply can cause the same code.","symptoms":"Possible loss of power or over/under-boost.","checks":"Check the fuse and supply voltage, then continuity of the control wire. Measure the component resistance (an open winding reads infinite).","repair":"Repair the confirmed wiring, connector, fuse or supply fault. Clean or replace the component only if it fails bench or scan-tool testing and the circuit is good. Clear the code and verify operation.","prevention":"Keep connectors dry and clean, repair chafed wiring early, and follow the maintenance schedule.","note":"A code points to a circuit or system; it does not prove the named part has failed. Test before replacing parts. Confirm the exact definition for the vehicle with OEM service information.","type":"generic"},
  "P0046": {"title":"Turbocharger/supercharger boost control solenoid A circuit range/performance","causes":"Component not responding as commanded (stuck, slow, binding or restricted) or a related mechanical/vacuum problem. Boost leaks, a sticking wastegate or actuator vacuum supply can cause the same code.","symptoms":"Possible loss of power or over/under-boost.","checks":"Command the component with a scan tool and watch the feedback value. Check for mechanical binding, carbon build-up, vacuum or hydraulic supply problems.","repair":"Repair the confirmed wiring, connector, fuse or supply fault. Clean or replace the component only if it fails bench or scan-tool testing and the circuit is good. Clear the code and verify operation.","prevention":"Keep connectors dry and clean, repair chafed wiring early, and follow the maintenance schedule.","note":"A code points to a circuit or system; it does not prove the named part has failed. Test before replacing parts. Confirm the exact definition for the vehicle with OEM service information.","type":"generic"},
  "P0047": {"title":"Turbocharger/supercharger boost control solenoid A circuit low","causes":"Control circuit voltage lower than expected: control wire shorted to ground, a shorted component winding, or a module driver fault. Whether \"low\" means shorted or open depends on how that circuit is designed, so check the wiring diagram. Boost leaks, a sticking wastegate or actuator vacuum supply can cause the same code.","symptoms":"Possible loss of power or over/under-boost.","checks":"Inspect the harness for chafing near the component. Measure the winding resistance (a shorted winding reads below specification). Check supply voltage and the circuit with the component unplugged.","repair":"Repair the confirmed wiring, connector, fuse or supply fault. Clean or replace the component only if it fails bench or scan-tool testing and the circuit is good. Clear the code and verify operation.","prevention":"Keep connectors dry and clean, repair chafed wiring early, and follow the maintenance schedule.","note":"A code points to a circuit or system; it does not prove the named part has failed. Test before replacing parts. Confirm the exact definition for the vehicle with OEM service information.","type":"generic"},
  "P0048": {"title":"Turbocharger/supercharger boost control solenoid A circuit high","causes":"Control circuit voltage higher than expected: control wire shorted to a power circuit, open ground path, or a module driver fault. Confirm the circuit design in the wiring diagram. Boost leaks, a sticking wastegate or actuator vacuum supply can cause the same code.","symptoms":"Possible loss of power or over/under-boost.","checks":"Inspect for a control wire touching a power circuit or an open ground. Test the circuit with the component unplugged and compare with specification.","repair":"Repair the confirmed wiring, connector, fuse or supply fault. Clean or replace the component only if it fails bench or scan-tool testing and the circuit is good. Clear the code and verify operation.","prevention":"Keep connectors dry and clean, repair chafed wiring early, and follow the maintenance schedule.","note":"A code points to a circuit or system; it does not prove the named part has failed. Test before replacing parts. Confirm the exact definition for the vehicle with OEM service information.","type":"generic"},
  "P0050": {"title":"HO2S Heater Control Circuit (Bank 2 Sensor 1)","causes":"Sensor heater, wiring, fuse, or connector fault.","symptoms":"Warning light and possible emissions issues.","checks":"Confirm engine bank layout; test heater circuit and power/ground.","prevention":"Repair exhaust wiring damage promptly.","note":"Generic reference description; verify against vehicle make/model/year and service information.","type":"generic","repair":"Fix leaks, wiring and connector faults first; replace a sensor or component only after it fails testing. Repair only what testing confirms (wiring, connector, leak or failed part), then clear the code and verify with a road test and live data."},
