  "P0126": {"title":"Insufficient coolant temperature for stable operation","causes":"Thermostat stuck open or missing, low coolant level, defective coolant temperature sensor, or cooling fan running constantly.","symptoms":"Poor cabin heat, engine warming slowly, higher fuel use.","checks":"Check the coolant level and the temperature gauge/scan-tool value, confirm the thermostat opens at its rated temperature, and check the ECT sensor against an infrared thermometer.","repair":"Replace a thermostat that is stuck open or a sensor that is out of range. Clear the code and verify warm-up time.","prevention":"Follow the manufacturer maintenance schedule and repair confirmed faults promptly.","note":"Never open a hot, pressurised cooling system. A code points to a circuit or system; it does not prove the named part has failed. Test before replacing parts. Confirm the exact definition for the vehicle with OEM service information.","type":"generic"},
  "P0127": {"title":"Intake air temperature too high","causes":"Heat soak, hot air being drawn from near the exhaust, a missing/damaged intake duct or air-box, IAT sensor reading high, or intercooler problems on turbo engines.","symptoms":"Possible reduced power or poor economy when hot.","checks":"Compare the IAT reading with actual air temperature, inspect air-intake ducting and heat shields, and check intercooler operation where fitted.","repair":"Restore ducting and shields, and replace the sensor only if it reads incorrectly. Clear the code and verify.","prevention":"Follow the manufacturer maintenance schedule and repair confirmed faults promptly.","note":"A code points to a circuit or system; it does not prove the named part has failed. Test before replacing parts. Confirm the exact definition for the vehicle with OEM service information.","type":"generic"},
