  "P0145": {"title":"Oxygen sensor circuit slow response Bank 1 Sensor 3","causes":"Sensor responding slowly: aged or contaminated sensor (silicone, oil, coolant, lead), exhaust leak, or wiring resistance.","symptoms":"Usually only a warning light; closed-loop fuel control may be affected, which can cause poor economy or higher emissions.","checks":"Watch the sensor switching rate on a scan tool or scope once warm; check for exhaust leaks and sensor contamination before replacing the sensor.","repair":"Repair wiring, connector or exhaust/intake leaks found. Replace the sensor only if it tests bad and the circuit is good, using the correct sensor for that position. Clear the code and verify with live data.","prevention":"Follow the manufacturer maintenance schedule and repair confirmed faults promptly.","note":"Exhaust parts get extremely hot; let them cool before handling. A code points to a circuit or system; it does not prove the named part has failed. Test before replacing parts. Confirm the exact definition for the vehicle with OEM service information.","type":"generic"},
  "P0146": {"title":"Oxygen sensor circuit no activity detected Bank 1 Sensor 3","causes":"No sensor activity detected: sensor unplugged, open signal circuit, failed sensor or heater circuit fault so the sensor never warms.","symptoms":"Usually only a warning light; closed-loop fuel control may be affected, which can cause poor economy or higher emissions.","checks":"Check connector and wiring, heater circuit codes and fuses, and whether the sensor voltage changes at all once the engine is warm.","repair":"Repair wiring, connector or exhaust/intake leaks found. Replace the sensor only if it tests bad and the circuit is good, using the correct sensor for that position. Clear the code and verify with live data.","prevention":"Follow the manufacturer maintenance schedule and repair confirmed faults promptly.","note":"Exhaust parts get extremely hot; let them cool before handling. A code points to a circuit or system; it does not prove the named part has failed. Test before replacing parts. Confirm the exact definition for the vehicle with OEM service information.","type":"generic"},
  "P0147": {"title":"Oxygen sensor heater circuit Bank 1 Sensor 3","causes":"Oxygen sensor heater, Bank 1 Sensor 3 heater control circuit: heater element, fuse or relay supply, ground, wiring, connector, or the module driver.","symptoms":"Usually a warning light only; slower closed-loop fuel control is possible.","checks":"Inspect the heater fuse and connector. Measure heater resistance against specification, and check supply voltage and the control signal.","repair":"Repair damaged wiring or connector. If the heater resistance is out of specification and the circuit tests good, replace the sensor/heater using the correct part for that position. Clear the code and verify heater operation.","prevention":"Keep connectors dry and clean, repair chafed wiring early, and follow the maintenance schedule.","note":"Exhaust parts get extremely hot; let them cool before handling. A code points to a circuit or system; it does not prove the named part has failed. Test before replacing parts. Confirm the exact definition for the vehicle with OEM service information.","type":"generic"},
