  "P0163": {"title":"Oxygen sensor circuit low voltage Bank 2 Sensor 3","causes":"Sensor voltage staying low: real lean condition (vacuum/intake leak, low fuel pressure), exhaust leak near the sensor, contaminated or aged sensor, or signal wire shorted to ground.","symptoms":"Usually only a warning light; closed-loop fuel control may be affected, which can cause poor economy or higher emissions.","checks":"Check fuel trims and look for intake or exhaust leaks first. Then check sensor wiring and connector, and the live sensor voltage response to a temporary propane/enrichment or a snap throttle (per service data).","repair":"Repair wiring, connector or exhaust/intake leaks found. Replace the sensor only if it tests bad and the circuit is good, using the correct sensor for that position. Clear the code and verify with live data.","prevention":"Follow the manufacturer maintenance schedule and repair confirmed faults promptly.","note":"Exhaust parts get extremely hot; let them cool before handling. A code points to a circuit or system; it does not prove the named part has failed. Test before replacing parts. Confirm the exact definition for the vehicle with OEM service information.","type":"generic"},
  "P0164": {"title":"Oxygen sensor circuit high voltage Bank 2 Sensor 3","causes":"Sensor voltage staying high: real rich condition (leaking injector, high fuel pressure, restricted air), contaminated sensor, or signal wire shorted to voltage.","symptoms":"Usually only a warning light; closed-loop fuel control may be affected, which can cause poor economy or higher emissions.","checks":"Check fuel trims, fuel pressure and air filter. Inspect sensor wiring and connector. Watch live sensor voltage response during snap-throttle or deceleration fuel cut-off.","repair":"Repair wiring, connector or exhaust/intake leaks found. Replace the sensor only if it tests bad and the circuit is good, using the correct sensor for that position. Clear the code and verify with live data.","prevention":"Follow the manufacturer maintenance schedule and repair confirmed faults promptly.","note":"Exhaust parts get extremely hot; let them cool before handling. A code points to a circuit or system; it does not prove the named part has failed. Test before replacing parts. Confirm the exact definition for the vehicle with OEM service information.","type":"generic"},
