  "P0335": {"title":"Crankshaft position sensor circuit","causes":"Crank sensor, wiring, connector or tone wheel.","symptoms":"Symptoms depend on the underlying fault; possible examples include a warning light, rough running, poor economy, hesitation, hard starting or reduced power.","checks":"Inspect connector and wiring; verify signal with suitable equipment.","prevention":"Secure harness away from heat/moving parts.","note":"Can cause no-start or stalling.","type":"generic","repair":"Repair the confirmed ignition, fuel or mechanical cause on the affected cylinder(s). Repair only what testing confirms (wiring, connector, leak or failed part), then clear the code and verify with a road test and live data."},
  "P0336": {"title":"Crankshaft position sensor range/performance","causes":"Sensor signal, wiring, tone wheel or mounting issue.","symptoms":"Symptoms depend on the underlying fault; possible examples include a warning light, rough running, poor economy, hesitation, hard starting or reduced power.","checks":"Inspect sensor and tone wheel; compare signal to service data.","prevention":"Maintain wiring and connectors.","note":"Generic code.","type":"generic","repair":"Repair the confirmed ignition, fuel or mechanical cause on the affected cylinder(s). Repair only what testing confirms (wiring, connector, leak or failed part), then clear the code and verify with a road test and live data."},
  "P0337": {"title":"Crankshaft position sensor A circuit low","causes":"Signal voltage lower than expected: signal wire shorted to ground, open supply (for Hall-effect sensors), or a faulty sensor.","symptoms":"Possible no-start, stalling, hard starting or rough running. A failing sensor often acts up when hot.","checks":"Check supply voltage and ground at the sensor, then inspect the signal wire.","repair":"Repair damaged wiring or connectors, secure or re-seat the sensor with the correct air gap, and replace the sensor only after the wiring and trigger wheel check good. If timing correlation is involved, verify mechanical timing before replacing sensors. Clear the code and verify.","prevention":"Keep connectors dry and clean, repair chafed wiring early, and follow the maintenance schedule.","note":"A code points to a circuit or system; it does not prove the named part has failed. Test before replacing parts. Confirm the exact definition for the vehicle with OEM service information.","type":"generic"},
  "P0338": {"title":"Crankshaft position sensor A circuit high","causes":"Signal voltage higher than expected: signal wire shorted to voltage, open ground, or a faulty sensor.","symptoms":"Possible no-start, stalling, hard starting or rough running. A failing sensor often acts up when hot.","checks":"Check sensor ground and the signal wire for a short to power.","repair":"Repair damaged wiring or connectors, secure or re-seat the sensor with the correct air gap, and replace the sensor only after the wiring and trigger wheel check good. If timing correlation is involved, verify mechanical timing before replacing sensors. Clear the code and verify.","prevention":"Keep connectors dry and clean, repair chafed wiring early, and follow the maintenance schedule.","note":"A code points to a circuit or system; it does not prove the named part has failed. Test before replacing parts. Confirm the exact definition for the vehicle with OEM service information.","type":"generic"},
