  "P0533": {"title":"A/C refrigerant pressure sensor A circuit high","causes":"Signal voltage higher than expected: signal wire shorted to voltage, open sensor ground, bad sensor, or a module fault. Low refrigerant charge can also give a low reading.","symptoms":"A/C compressor may not engage; cooling performance may be poor.","checks":"Key on, engine off: check sensor ground (should be near 0 V drop) and reference voltage; inspect for a signal wire touching a power circuit. If several sensors show high-voltage codes together, suspect a shared ground problem.","repair":"Repair the wiring, connector or shared reference/ground fault found by testing. Replace the sensor only after the wiring and the real condition have been ruled out. Clear the code and verify with live data.","prevention":"Keep connectors dry and clean, repair chafed wiring early, and follow the maintenance schedule.","note":"A/C refrigerant is under pressure; use a licensed procedure and equipment. A code points to a circuit or system; it does not prove the named part has failed. Test before replacing parts. Confirm the exact definition for the vehicle with OEM service information.","type":"generic"},
  "P0534": {"title":"A/C refrigerant charge loss","causes":"Low refrigerant from a leak, or a faulty pressure sensor or switch.","symptoms":"Weak or no cooling; compressor may cycle often or not engage.","checks":"Check A/C pressures with manifold gauges and inspect for leaks with a UV dye or electronic leak detector.","repair":"Repair the leak and recharge with a licensed procedure. Verify cooling.","prevention":"Follow the manufacturer maintenance schedule and repair confirmed faults promptly.","note":"A/C refrigerant is under pressure; use proper equipment and follow environmental rules. A code points to a circuit or system; it does not prove the named part has failed. Test before replacing parts. Confirm the exact definition for the vehicle with OEM service information.","type":"generic"},
  "P0544": {"title":"Exhaust gas temperature sensor circuit Bank 1 Sensor 1","causes":"Exhaust gas temperature (EGT) sensor, Bank 1 Sensor 1 signal or ground wiring, connector, the sensor itself, or the module input. Mostly used on diesel and some turbocharged engines.","symptoms":"Possible reduced power or regeneration problems on diesel particulate filter systems.","checks":"Compare the scan-tool temperature with a known temperature (on a fully cold-soaked engine, IAT, coolant and ambient readings should be close). Inspect the connector for corrosion or water. Measure sensor resistance and compare with the temperature/resistance chart in service data.","repair":"Repair the damaged wiring or connector found during testing. Replace the sensor only if its resistance does not match the specification and the wiring tests good. Clear the code and verify the reading with the scan tool.","prevention":"Keep connectors dry and clean, repair chafed wiring early, and follow the maintenance schedule.","note":"Exhaust parts get extremely hot; let them cool before handling. A code points to a circuit or system; it does not prove the named part has failed. Test before replacing parts. Confirm the exact definition for the vehicle with OEM service information.","type":"generic"},
