  "P0652": {"title":"Sensor reference voltage B circuit low","causes":"Sensor reference voltage B fault: reference voltage pulled low: short to ground on the reference circuit, a failed sensor pulling the reference down, or a module fault. Several sensors normally share one reference, so one failed sensor or wiring short can set several codes.","symptoms":"Several sensors may read wrong at once; possible limp mode, rough running, or reduced power.","checks":"Find which sensors share this reference in the wiring diagram. With the ignition on, measure the reference voltage (about 5 V on most designs), then unplug the sensors one at a time to see if the voltage returns.","repair":"Repair the shorted or open reference wiring, or replace the shorted sensor found; clear the codes and verify the reference voltage.","prevention":"Follow the manufacturer maintenance schedule and repair confirmed faults promptly.","note":"A code points to a circuit or system; it does not prove the named part has failed. Test before replacing parts. Confirm the exact definition for the vehicle with OEM service information.","type":"generic"},
  "P0653": {"title":"Sensor reference voltage B circuit high","causes":"Sensor reference voltage B fault: reference voltage too high: short to voltage on the reference circuit or a module fault. Several sensors normally share one reference, so one failed sensor or wiring short can set several codes.","symptoms":"Several sensors may read wrong at once; possible limp mode, rough running, or reduced power.","checks":"Find which sensors share this reference in the wiring diagram. With the ignition on, measure the reference voltage (about 5 V on most designs), then unplug the sensors one at a time to see if the voltage returns.","repair":"Repair the shorted or open reference wiring, or replace the shorted sensor found; clear the codes and verify the reference voltage.","prevention":"Follow the manufacturer maintenance schedule and repair confirmed faults promptly.","note":"A code points to a circuit or system; it does not prove the named part has failed. Test before replacing parts. Confirm the exact definition for the vehicle with OEM service information.","type":"generic"},
  "P0657": {"title":"Actuator supply voltage A circuit/open","causes":"Open circuit: blown fuse, open supply or control wire, poor connector contact, open coil/winding, or a failed module driver.","symptoms":"Possible limp mode or loss of actuator functions.","checks":"Check the fuse and supply voltage, then continuity of the control wire. Measure the component resistance (an open winding reads infinite).","repair":"Repair the confirmed wiring, connector, fuse or supply fault. Clean or replace the component only if it fails bench or scan-tool testing and the circuit is good. Clear the code and verify operation.","prevention":"Keep connectors dry and clean, repair chafed wiring early, and follow the maintenance schedule.","note":"A code points to a circuit or system; it does not prove the named part has failed. Test before replacing parts. Confirm the exact definition for the vehicle with OEM service information.","type":"generic"},
  "P0658": {"title":"Actuator supply voltage A circuit low","causes":"Control circuit voltage lower than expected: control wire shorted to ground, a shorted component winding, or a module driver fault. Whether \"low\" means shorted or open depends on how that circuit is designed, so check the wiring diagram.","symptoms":"Possible limp mode or loss of actuator functions.","checks":"Inspect the harness for chafing near the component. Measure the winding resistance (a shorted winding reads below specification). Check supply voltage and the circuit with the component unplugged.","repair":"Repair the confirmed wiring, connector, fuse or supply fault. Clean or replace the component only if it fails bench or scan-tool testing and the circuit is good. Clear the code and verify operation.","prevention":"Keep connectors dry and clean, repair chafed wiring early, and follow the maintenance schedule.","note":"A code points to a circuit or system; it does not prove the named part has failed. Test before replacing parts. Confirm the exact definition for the vehicle with OEM service information.","type":"generic"},
