  "P2135": {"title":"Throttle/pedal position sensor A/B voltage correlation","causes":"The two throttle sensor signals do not agree: throttle body sensor fault, wiring resistance on one signal, or a shared reference or ground problem.","symptoms":"Reduced power or limp-home mode.","checks":"Compare throttle sensor A and B live values (they should mirror each other), check reference voltage and ground, and inspect the wiring.","repair":"Repair the wiring or replace the throttle body (sensors are usually not serviced separately).","prevention":"Follow the manufacturer maintenance schedule and repair confirmed faults promptly.","note":"Throttle faults can cause unpredictable throttle response; avoid driving if the engine surges or the throttle sticks. A code points to a circuit or system; it does not prove the named part has failed. Test before replacing parts. Confirm the exact definition for the vehicle with OEM service information.","type":"generic"},
  "P2138": {"title":"Throttle/pedal position sensor D/E voltage correlation","causes":"The two accelerator-pedal sensors do not agree: pedal assembly fault, wiring resistance on one signal, or a shared reference or ground problem.","symptoms":"Reduced power or limp-home mode.","checks":"Compare pedal sensor D and E live values while pressing the pedal slowly, and check reference, ground and wiring.","repair":"Repair the wiring or replace the pedal assembly.","prevention":"Follow the manufacturer maintenance schedule and repair confirmed faults promptly.","note":"Throttle faults can cause unpredictable throttle response; avoid driving if the engine surges or the throttle sticks. A code points to a circuit or system; it does not prove the named part has failed. Test before replacing parts. Confirm the exact definition for the vehicle with OEM service information.","type":"generic"},
  "P2176": {"title":"Throttle actuator control system - idle position not learned","causes":"The throttle idle position has not been learned after battery disconnect, throttle body replacement or cleaning.","symptoms":"Unstable or high idle, or stalling.","checks":"Check for other throttle codes, then perform the manufacturer's throttle relearn (usually with a scan tool).","repair":"Complete the relearn procedure, then clear the code and verify idle.","prevention":"Follow the manufacturer maintenance schedule and repair confirmed faults promptly.","note":"Throttle faults can cause unpredictable throttle response; avoid driving if the engine surges or the throttle sticks. A code points to a circuit or system; it does not prove the named part has failed. Test before replacing parts. Confirm the exact definition for the vehicle with OEM service information.","type":"generic"},
  "P2177": {"title":"System too lean off idle Bank 1","causes":"Mixture too lean off idle on Bank 1: intake or vacuum leak, weak fuel delivery, dirty MAF, exhaust leak before the O2 sensor, or leaking injector seals.","symptoms":"Rough idle, hesitation, or poor power.","checks":"Check short- and long-term fuel trims at idle and at 2500 rpm; look for leaks, check fuel pressure, and inspect the MAF/MAP sensors.","repair":"Repair the confirmed fuel, air or exhaust leak fault. Clear the code and re-check trims.","prevention":"Follow the manufacturer maintenance schedule and repair confirmed faults promptly.","note":"Fuel is flammable: no flames or sparks, and relieve fuel pressure using the service procedure before opening any fuel line. A code points to a circuit or system; it does not prove the named part has failed. Test before replacing parts. Confirm the exact definition for the vehicle with OEM service information.","type":"generic"},
  "P2178": {"title":"System too rich off idle Bank 1","causes":"Mixture too rich off idle on Bank 1: leaking injector, high fuel pressure, restricted air intake, faulty MAF, or purge valve stuck open.","symptoms":"Rough idle, fuel smell, black smoke or poor economy.","checks":"Check short- and long-term fuel trims at idle and at 2500 rpm; look for leaks, check fuel pressure, and inspect the MAF/MAP sensors.","repair":"Repair the confirmed fuel, air or exhaust leak fault. Clear the code and re-check trims.","prevention":"Follow the manufacturer maintenance schedule and repair confirmed faults promptly.","note":"Fuel is flammable: no flames or sparks, and relieve fuel pressure using the service procedure before opening any fuel line. A code points to a circuit or system; it does not prove the named part has failed. Test before replacing parts. Confirm the exact definition for the vehicle with OEM service information.","type":"generic"},
