  "P2180": {"title":"System too rich off idle Bank 2","causes":"Mixture too rich off idle on Bank 2: leaking injector, high fuel pressure, restricted air intake, faulty MAF, or purge valve stuck open.","symptoms":"Rough idle, fuel smell, black smoke or poor economy.","checks":"Check short- and long-term fuel trims at idle and at 2500 rpm; look for leaks, check fuel pressure, and inspect the MAF/MAP sensors.","repair":"Repair the confirmed fuel, air or exhaust leak fault. Clear the code and re-check trims.","prevention":"Follow the manufacturer maintenance schedule and repair confirmed faults promptly.","note":"Fuel is flammable: no flames or sparks, and relieve fuel pressure using the service procedure before opening any fuel line. A code points to a circuit or system; it does not prove the named part has failed. Test before replacing parts. Confirm the exact definition for the vehicle with OEM service information.","type":"generic"},
  "P2182": {"title":"Engine coolant temperature sensor 2 circuit","causes":"Engine coolant temperature (ECT) sensor 2 signal or ground wiring, connector, the sensor itself, or the module input. ECT 2 location and purpose (fan control, gauge, or second reading) vary by engine.","symptoms":"Possible cooling-fan or temperature-gauge behaviour that does not match actual engine temperature.","checks":"Compare the scan-tool temperature with a known temperature (on a fully cold-soaked engine, IAT, coolant and ambient readings should be close). Inspect the connector for corrosion or water. Measure sensor resistance and compare with the temperature/resistance chart in service data. Check that the coolant level is correct and the sensor is in the coolant, not in an air pocket.","repair":"Repair the damaged wiring or connector found during testing. Replace the sensor only if its resistance does not match the specification and the wiring tests good. Clear the code and verify the reading with the scan tool.","prevention":"Keep connectors dry and clean, repair chafed wiring early, and follow the maintenance schedule.","note":"Never open a hot, pressurised cooling system. A code points to a circuit or system; it does not prove the named part has failed. Test before replacing parts. Confirm the exact definition for the vehicle with OEM service information.","type":"generic"},
  "P2183": {"title":"Engine coolant temperature sensor 2 circuit range/performance","causes":"Reading does not agree with other temperature data or is stuck/slow: drifting or contaminated sensor, high resistance in the connector or wiring, or a real condition that makes the temperature wrong. ECT 2 location and purpose (fan control, gauge, or second reading) vary by engine.","symptoms":"Possible cooling-fan or temperature-gauge behaviour that does not match actual engine temperature.","checks":"Compare with other temperature readings on a cold-soaked engine, then watch the value rise while warming up. Check sensor resistance and connector condition. Check that the coolant level is correct and the sensor is in the coolant, not in an air pocket.","repair":"Repair the damaged wiring or connector found during testing. Replace the sensor only if its resistance does not match the specification and the wiring tests good. Clear the code and verify the reading with the scan tool.","prevention":"Keep connectors dry and clean, repair chafed wiring early, and follow the maintenance schedule.","note":"Never open a hot, pressurised cooling system. A code points to a circuit or system; it does not prove the named part has failed. Test before replacing parts. Confirm the exact definition for the vehicle with OEM service information.","type":"generic"},
  "P2184": {"title":"Engine coolant temperature sensor 2 circuit low","causes":"Signal voltage lower than expected, so the module sees an unrealistically HOT reading: signal wire shorted to ground, water or corrosion in the connector, or an internally shorted sensor. ECT 2 location and purpose (fan control, gauge, or second reading) vary by engine.","symptoms":"Possible cooling-fan or temperature-gauge behaviour that does not match actual engine temperature.","checks":"Scan-tool reading will usually show an extreme high temperature. Unplug the sensor: the reading should swing to the opposite extreme; if it does not, suspect wiring or the module input. Check for a signal wire rubbing on metal. Measure sensor resistance against the service chart. Check that the coolant level is correct and the sensor is in the coolant, not in an air pocket.","repair":"Repair the damaged wiring or connector found during testing. Replace the sensor only if its resistance does not match the specification and the wiring tests good. Clear the code and verify the reading with the scan tool.","prevention":"Keep connectors dry and clean, repair chafed wiring early, and follow the maintenance schedule.","note":"Never open a hot, pressurised cooling system. A code points to a circuit or system; it does not prove the named part has failed. Test before replacing parts. Confirm the exact definition for the vehicle with OEM service information.","type":"generic"},
  "P2185": {"title":"Engine coolant temperature sensor 2 circuit high","causes":"Sensor 2 circuit open/high resistance, connector issue, wiring fault, or sensor fault; exact layout varies by vehicle.","symptoms":"Temperature data may be implausible; cooling-fan behavior or warning light may be affected.","checks":"Confirm the OEM definition and sensor location for the exact engine. Inspect connector/wiring and compare live data; test circuit using service specifications.","prevention":"Never open a hot pressurized cooling system. Do not assume sensor 2 location is universal.","note":"Vehicle-specific details may vary; confirm the OEM definition and service procedure before diagnosis.","type":"generic","repair":"Repair only what testing confirms (wiring, connector, leak or failed part), then clear the code and verify with a road test and live data."},
  "P2186": {"title":"Engine coolant temperature sensor 2 circuit intermittent/erratic","causes":"Brief signal drop-outs: loose, corroded or chafed connector or wiring, or a sensor that fails with heat or vibration. ECT 2 location and purpose (fan control, gauge, or second reading) vary by engine.","symptoms":"Possible cooling-fan or temperature-gauge behaviour that does not match actual engine temperature.","checks":"Watch the live reading while wiggling the harness and connector; look for sudden jumps. Inspect the connector pins for spread terminals or corrosion. Check that the coolant level is correct and the sensor is in the coolant, not in an air pocket.","repair":"Repair the damaged wiring or connector found during testing. Replace the sensor only if its resistance does not match the specification and the wiring tests good. Clear the code and verify the reading with the scan tool.","prevention":"Keep connectors dry and clean, repair chafed wiring early, and follow the maintenance schedule.","note":"Never open a hot, pressurised cooling system. A code points to a circuit or system; it does not prove the named part has failed. Test before replacing parts. Confirm the exact definition for the vehicle with OEM service information.","type":"generic"},
