  "P2195": {"title":"O2 sensor signal stuck lean Bank 1 Sensor 1","causes":"The sensor reports a lean mixture that the fuel control cannot correct; this can be a real lean condition (vacuum or exhaust leak, low fuel pressure) or a sensor/circuit fault. This code applies to Bank 1 Sensor 1. Wide-range (air-fuel ratio) sensors use pumping-current circuits, so the wiring, connector, the sensor, or the module driver can all be involved.","symptoms":"Warning light; fuel control and emissions may be affected, which can cause poor economy or rough running.","checks":"Check for related heater and fuel-trim codes. Inspect the sensor connector and wiring (these sensors are sensitive to resistance and moisture). Use a scan tool to read the sensor's live air-fuel ratio or current/voltage data and compare with expected values in service information.","repair":"Repair wiring or connector faults and exhaust/intake leaks. Replace the sensor only after the circuit and the real mixture condition have been checked; use the exact sensor type specified for that vehicle. Clear the code and verify.","prevention":"Follow the manufacturer maintenance schedule and repair confirmed faults promptly.","note":"Exhaust parts get extremely hot; let them cool before handling. Wide-range sensor definitions (P22xx) are generic but the sensor layout varies by manufacturer. A code points to a circuit or system; it does not prove the named part has failed. Test before replacing parts. Confirm the exact definition for the vehicle with OEM service information.","type":"generic"},
  "P2196": {"title":"O2 sensor signal stuck rich Bank 1 Sensor 1","causes":"The sensor reports a rich mixture that the fuel control cannot correct; this can be a real rich condition (leaking injector, high fuel pressure) or a sensor/circuit fault. This code applies to Bank 1 Sensor 1. Wide-range (air-fuel ratio) sensors use pumping-current circuits, so the wiring, connector, the sensor, or the module driver can all be involved.","symptoms":"Warning light; fuel control and emissions may be affected, which can cause poor economy or rough running.","checks":"Check for related heater and fuel-trim codes. Inspect the sensor connector and wiring (these sensors are sensitive to resistance and moisture). Use a scan tool to read the sensor's live air-fuel ratio or current/voltage data and compare with expected values in service information.","repair":"Repair wiring or connector faults and exhaust/intake leaks. Replace the sensor only after the circuit and the real mixture condition have been checked; use the exact sensor type specified for that vehicle. Clear the code and verify.","prevention":"Follow the manufacturer maintenance schedule and repair confirmed faults promptly.","note":"Exhaust parts get extremely hot; let them cool before handling. Wide-range sensor definitions (P22xx) are generic but the sensor layout varies by manufacturer. A code points to a circuit or system; it does not prove the named part has failed. Test before replacing parts. Confirm the exact definition for the vehicle with OEM service information.","type":"generic"},
