  "P2226": {"title":"Barometric pressure sensor A circuit","causes":"Barometric pressure sensor signal, reference-voltage or ground wiring, connector, the sensor, or the module input. The sensor may be inside the ECM, on the intake or part of the MAP sensor; a real altitude or restriction change can also change the reading.","symptoms":"Possible poor performance or incorrect fuel delivery, especially at altitude changes.","checks":"Check the live scan-tool value against what the operating condition should give. Back-probe reference voltage (about 5 V on most designs), ground and signal; inspect the connector.","repair":"Repair the wiring, connector or shared reference/ground fault found by testing. Replace the sensor only after the wiring and the real condition have been ruled out. Clear the code and verify with live data.","prevention":"Keep connectors dry and clean, repair chafed wiring early, and follow the maintenance schedule.","note":"A code points to a circuit or system; it does not prove the named part has failed. Test before replacing parts. Confirm the exact definition for the vehicle with OEM service information.","type":"generic"},
  "P2227": {"title":"Barometric pressure sensor A circuit range/performance","causes":"Sensor signal does not agree with the expected value or with related sensors: sensor drift or contamination, a real mechanical, vacuum or pressure problem, or high resistance in the wiring. The sensor may be inside the ECM, on the intake or part of the MAP sensor; a real altitude or restriction change can also change the reading.","symptoms":"Possible poor performance or incorrect fuel delivery, especially at altitude changes.","checks":"Compare the live value with the expected value for the operating condition and with related sensors (see service data). Rule out the real condition (leaks, blockage, mechanical wear) before condemning the sensor.","repair":"Repair the wiring, connector or shared reference/ground fault found by testing. Replace the sensor only after the wiring and the real condition have been ruled out. Clear the code and verify with live data.","prevention":"Keep connectors dry and clean, repair chafed wiring early, and follow the maintenance schedule.","note":"A code points to a circuit or system; it does not prove the named part has failed. Test before replacing parts. Confirm the exact definition for the vehicle with OEM service information.","type":"generic"},
  "P2228": {"title":"Barometric pressure sensor A circuit low","causes":"Signal voltage lower than expected: signal wire shorted to ground, open 5-volt reference supply, bad sensor, or a module sensor-supply fault. The sensor may be inside the ECM, on the intake or part of the MAP sensor; a real altitude or restriction change can also change the reading.","symptoms":"Possible poor performance or incorrect fuel delivery, especially at altitude changes.","checks":"Key on, engine off: measure reference voltage and ground at the sensor connector, then the signal. If several sensors set low-voltage codes together, suspect a shared reference-voltage or ground fault rather than each sensor.","repair":"Repair the wiring, connector or shared reference/ground fault found by testing. Replace the sensor only after the wiring and the real condition have been ruled out. Clear the code and verify with live data.","prevention":"Keep connectors dry and clean, repair chafed wiring early, and follow the maintenance schedule.","note":"A code points to a circuit or system; it does not prove the named part has failed. Test before replacing parts. Confirm the exact definition for the vehicle with OEM service information.","type":"generic"},
  "P2229": {"title":"Barometric pressure sensor A circuit high","causes":"Signal voltage higher than expected: signal wire shorted to voltage, open sensor ground, bad sensor, or a module fault. The sensor may be inside the ECM, on the intake or part of the MAP sensor; a real altitude or restriction change can also change the reading.","symptoms":"Possible poor performance or incorrect fuel delivery, especially at altitude changes.","checks":"Key on, engine off: check sensor ground (should be near 0 V drop) and reference voltage; inspect for a signal wire touching a power circuit. If several sensors show high-voltage codes together, suspect a shared ground problem.","repair":"Repair the wiring, connector or shared reference/ground fault found by testing. Replace the sensor only after the wiring and the real condition have been ruled out. Clear the code and verify with live data.","prevention":"Keep connectors dry and clean, repair chafed wiring early, and follow the maintenance schedule.","note":"A code points to a circuit or system; it does not prove the named part has failed. Test before replacing parts. Confirm the exact definition for the vehicle with OEM service information.","type":"generic"},
  "P2230": {"title":"Barometric pressure sensor A circuit intermittent/erratic","causes":"Brief signal drop-outs: loose or corroded connector, chafed wiring, or a sensor that fails with heat or vibration. The sensor may be inside the ECM, on the intake or part of the MAP sensor; a real altitude or restriction change can also change the reading.","symptoms":"Possible poor performance or incorrect fuel delivery, especially at altitude changes.","checks":"Watch the live value while wiggling the harness and connector; look for sudden jumps. Inspect terminals.","repair":"Repair the wiring, connector or shared reference/ground fault found by testing. Replace the sensor only after the wiring and the real condition have been ruled out. Clear the code and verify with live data.","prevention":"Keep connectors dry and clean, repair chafed wiring early, and follow the maintenance schedule.","note":"A code points to a circuit or system; it does not prove the named part has failed. Test before replacing parts. Confirm the exact definition for the vehicle with OEM service information.","type":"generic"},
