  "P2300": {"title":"Ignition coil A primary control circuit low","causes":"Control circuit voltage lower than expected: control wire shorted to ground, a shorted component winding, or a module driver fault. Whether \"low\" means shorted or open depends on how that circuit is designed, so check the wiring diagram. Cylinder assignment of coil A varies; use the service manual.","symptoms":"Possible misfire, rough running or reduced power on the cylinder(s) served by that coil.","checks":"Inspect the harness for chafing near the component. Measure the winding resistance (a shorted winding reads below specification). Check supply voltage and the circuit with the component unplugged.","repair":"Repair the confirmed wiring, connector, fuse or supply fault. Clean or replace the component only if it fails bench or scan-tool testing and the circuit is good. Clear the code and verify operation.","prevention":"Keep connectors dry and clean, repair chafed wiring early, and follow the maintenance schedule.","note":"Ignition systems carry high voltage; never touch coils or leads with the engine running. A code points to a circuit or system; it does not prove the named part has failed. Test before replacing parts. Confirm the exact definition for the vehicle with OEM service information.","type":"generic"},
  "P2301": {"title":"Ignition coil A primary control circuit high","causes":"Control circuit voltage higher than expected: control wire shorted to a power circuit, open ground path, or a module driver fault. Confirm the circuit design in the wiring diagram. Cylinder assignment of coil A varies; use the service manual.","symptoms":"Possible misfire, rough running or reduced power on the cylinder(s) served by that coil.","checks":"Inspect for a control wire touching a power circuit or an open ground. Test the circuit with the component unplugged and compare with specification.","repair":"Repair the confirmed wiring, connector, fuse or supply fault. Clean or replace the component only if it fails bench or scan-tool testing and the circuit is good. Clear the code and verify operation.","prevention":"Keep connectors dry and clean, repair chafed wiring early, and follow the maintenance schedule.","note":"Ignition systems carry high voltage; never touch coils or leads with the engine running. A code points to a circuit or system; it does not prove the named part has failed. Test before replacing parts. Confirm the exact definition for the vehicle with OEM service information.","type":"generic"},
